within Pharmacolibrary.Drugs.R_RespiratorySystem.R03A_AdrenergicsInhalants.R03AK10_VilanterolAndFluticasone;
model Vilanterol_Kempsford2014_PD_hr
  "linear exposure-response sweep — Kempsford_2014 :: HR (maximal heart rate)"
  extends Pharmacolibrary.Pharmacodynamic.LinearSweep(
    E0 = 23.6,      // [beats min−1]
    slope = 17300000,  // [(1)/(kg/m3); beats min−1 pg−1 ml × 1e+09]
    exposure_per_s = 1e-09);   // 1 pg ml−1 per second
  // slope_unit_assumed: slope unit 'beats min−1 pg−1 ml' read as beats min−1 per pg ml−1
  annotation(experiment(StartTime = 0, StopTime = 1000, Interval = 2),
    Documentation(info = "<html><body><h4>Vilanterol_Kempsford2014_PD_hr</h4><p>The steady-state exposure-response curve of Kempsford R et al., <i>British journal of clinical pharmacology 77(3):466-479</i> 2014 (<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1111/bcp.12243\">doi:10.1111/bcp.12243</a>) — maximal heart rate [beats min−1] against VI pg ml−1 — obtained by sweeping the driver instead of simulating a dose. Model family: linear.</p><h4>The parameters, in the paper's terms</h4><pre>  E0    =       23.6 beats min−1 drug-free response (baseline) (Intercept, Q324)
  slope =     0.0173 beats min−1 pg−1 ml change in response per unit of exposure (Slope, Q335)</pre><p>The algebra is <a href=\"modelica://Pharmacolibrary.Pharmacodynamic.LinearSweep\">LinearSweep</a>; this model only supplies the numbers. The x axis is one pg ml−1 of VI per second of simulated time (exposure is in kg/m3): there is no state, no dose and no kinetics — plot <code>response</code> against <code>exposure</code> (or <code>time</code>).</p><h4>What it reproduces</h4><pre>  exposure = 0         response 23.6   (drug-free)</pre></body></html>", revisions = "<html><body><ul><li>11/2026 generated by pharmacolibrary-dev export.pharmacolibrary_models from Kempsford_2014: parameters extracted from the paper by the pharmacolibrary-dev pipeline, model built by its engineer, reviewed: simulated</li></ul></body></html>"));
end Vilanterol_Kempsford2014_PD_hr;
